Charles E Taylor IV <tomalek@mindspring.com> wrote:
> In article <39760BC1.791057AF@okstate.edu>,
> 	Mathew Lee <lmathew@okstate.edu> writes:

> [Discussion of the Multia]

>> ARC is old, and outdated (for NT)....SRM, in my opinion, is the way to go

> The Multia is old and outdated as well.  As long as the system boots
> Linux properly, is there any real advantage to using SRM over ARC on a
> system like a Multia if you're booting from the hard drive?

ARC/MILO:

	* needs an MS-DOS partition table
	* needs a small MS-DOS partition to boot from
	* syncs with low frequency monitors (always 640x480 res)
	* MILO keeps a compressed version of itself in memory so that
		you can reboot back to the MILO prompt
	* You can jump in at the MILO prompt and boot alternate kernels
	* MILO can do `ls' so you can see the files on the hard drive

SRM/ABOOT:

	* needs a BSD-disklabel style partition table
	* needs the first cylinder spare to boot from (usually)
	* syncs with a range of monitor frequencies depending on jumper
		settings (but some SRM versions are broken on some settings)
	* does not stay memory resident, reboot goes to SRM prompt
	* You can jump in at the aboot prompt and boot alternate kernels
	* aboot cannot do `ls'

ARC/ABOOT:

	* This does not work

SRM/MILO:

	* haven't tried this

One other ``issue'' is that the aboot source is fairly self-contained
and moderately up to date. The MILO source requires that you compile it
alongside a fairly old linux kernel so if you like to compile everything
from source then aboot is easier to compile.

	- Tel



