Sorry to object but using non-ECC memory is a bad idea.  Eventually the
memory has some error and you'll never know it.  Make sure that if you
use standard memory you are using an over-specified power supply and a
line-filtering UPS.  A power sag can easily cause single-bit errors,
possibly right in the middle of writing to a file or within kernel
memory, in which case you're probably going to lose data.  Keep the
system cool, too.

I.e. I wouldn't recommend getting anything but real ECC RAM.  It's not
that expensive and a hell of a lot cheaper than rebuilding a system that
was corrupted.

Regards,

Tony Hammitt


