Software: RH 6.2 w/Linux kernel 2.2.16, XFree86 3.3.6, ctwm 3.5.2

I'm logged in the Alpha as user when suddenly X freezes, leaving
the screen semi-drawn sometimes (if I'm switching between virtual
desktops, for example). Control-Alt-FX does not change consoles
and thus I log in as root from another machine. I can get into
the Alpha without problem. I then kill a couple of things which
are known to cause problems (e.g. Netscape), but X remains frozen
(Netscape, for example, does not disappear from the screen). OK,
kill the windows manager (ctwm). Nothing. Kill X. Nothing, the
screen remains as it was when it froze. No more mister nice guy:
as root I remotely kill all the processes owned by me
(user). Still, the screen is locked up. I Control-Alt-Backspace
on the Alpha without much hope. Nothing. I Control-Alt-Delete
(which should shutdown). Still frozen. For all intents and
purposes the screen, mouse and keyboard are useless. Defeated, I
return to my root session on the other machine and shutdown the
Alpha remotely. I restart and all is well until the next lockup
(usually about 10 days later).

/var/log doesn't show anything strange, although
Netscape gives the following messages:

Jul 19 19:28:45 chibi kernel: <sc 0(84,190,11ffffae8)><sc
53(8,190,11ffffae8)><sc 0(17,19d,11ffffae8)>
<sc53(8,19d,11ffffae8)>set_program_attributes(12000000 d8c000 14000000 45d1d0)

These show up all the time, though, and don't seem to have any
relation with the lockups (it once froze overnight while xlock
was running).

Could it be a hardware issue?  The Alpha used to run Windows NT
without problems, although it was never used as intensely as I do
now under Linux (4 virtual screens, between 5 and 10 windows in
each one). A buggy X11 driver for the Matrox, perhaps?

In any case my real question is as follows: is there anything I
can try before giving up and shutting down? I don't know what
else to kill, or how to "knudge" the screen so that it drops me
back into text mode (i.e. after X is killed). If there is nothing
I can do (at least software-wise), should I make it a habit of
restarting X every week or so?

Thoughts, ideas, comments, suggestions...? Please? :)


