> Motherboard is not:
> - letting me boot by MILO from the HD. Its not recognizing the HD. Only
>   get MILO to boot by floppy disk.

ARC and Alpha BIOS are VERY touchy when it comes to the FAT partition. I
recomment using the in BIOS tool for creating it.

Here is how one of mine is setup:

rbf@mustang:~$ df
Filesystem           1k-blocks      Used Available Use% Mounted on
/dev/sda8              1189183    681967    445777  60% /
/dev/sdb5              2029267   1528295    396065  79% /usr
/dev/sda7               576825    294759    252269  54% /usr/src
/dev/sda5                 1427       438       989  31% /bp

sda5 is a FAT partition where MILO and loadlin.exe are located. Is your
filesystem structure similar?

> - recognizing the floppy drive as a 1.44 MB. Kernel is reporting it as  
>   an 2.88MB. I know that it is a 1.44 MB.
>       - This problem lets me read other floppies formatted on a
>         1.44 MB floppy. It does not let other computers to read
>         floppies made by this computer.

This is a kernel bug that has been around as long as Linux has been
ported to Alpha. It has been discussed MANY times on the Red Hat AXP
list. If you like you can search that, and other, list archives via
AlphaLinux.org (in the docs section).

Basically it would require a lot of work to fix it. Everything works
correctly, but the kernel assumes the floppy is 2.88MB (which some of
the older Alphas have). When working with floppies (formating) you
can simply specify the size. i.e. 'superformat /dev/fd0u1440'

> Motherboard does work beyond this with exception of the video card and
> network card. The video card is a Voodoo3 2000 PCI. I know that its not
> really supported. This machine is intended to only be command line
> unless I can get a supported card for a not a lot of money. The network
> card is giving me oversized ethernet frames. I can try to log into
> another computer via ftp. I can get as far as putting my login name in
> and then nothing.

What is the Network card? You are right about the video card...
apparently it uses some x86 BIOS calls that are not emulated by the
BIOS on Alphas (yet?)


HTH,

Ron
