> boot hdc2:/boot/vmlinux-2.2.16-gz root=/dev/hdc2
>
> I receive:
>
> Partition check:
> request_module[block-major-8]: Root fs not mounted
> VFS: Cannot open root device 08:02
> Kernel panic: VFS: Unable to mount root fs on 08:02
>
> Does anybody know what does it mean?
> Additionally, there is no rdev command in alpha version of RH. How could
> I check what is root device for my kernel?

You're the second person to have reported this problem 8/
From the above I'm fairly sure that you still use AlphaBIOS and MILO to
boot
and this is the combination that I've seen reported before 8/

I have to admit I'm not in a position to try this combo unless someone can
remind me where the latest AlphaBIOS ROM image is and I'll check over the
weekend
I myself use a sx164 at home and a few ds20's at work and all are loaded
with SRM
and aboot

With regard to the rdev,...
rdev fiddles with byte offsets in the kernel to drop in the boot device. In
the x86 universe
this is lowbyte highbyte order eg SCSI partition 3 would be 03 08 at (if
memory serves
me correctly) byte offsets 513 & 514.

The alpha boot process , however, does non of this mucking around. It
relies on the
BIOS boot process to bootstrap something with more intelligence
ie SRM -> aboot -> kernel proper
or AlphaBIOS -> Milo -> kernel proper

It is then the responsibility of milo/aboot to provide the next part of the
boot sequence
(load and start kernel) with the details of where it's rootfs if going to
be found and this is
normally passed in via the flag 'root=/dev/XYZ'. For the Alpha, there is no
default boot
partition or method to set it as the x86 kernels allow. Maybe this is
something that needs
addresseing, or maybe a new methadology needs exploring.

All this is fine but doesn't help YOU. Now then, There are newer AlphaBIOS
ROM images
around if you feel daring and would like to upgrade somewhere on the
www.compaq.com site
and there is a newer milo available (see www.alphalinux.org for details)

Your message from above is about the kernel trying to open the rootfs on a
/dev/sda2
the problem is that it needs the driver which has been compiled as a module
and is held
on the rootfs as a file.
THIS is bad news for you because your boot line is for an IDE disk, not
SCSI

Myself I ditched AlphaBIOS a long time ago an use the latest SRM (again
from
www.compaq.com somewhere) and that latest aboot
(http://www.debian.org/~dhd/aboot/)
I'm successfully running 2.2.16/17 2.,4.0-testX on the Alpha using these.

The SRM howto looks like it needs a lot of polishing. I'm dire at tracking
down manuals and I
wasn't able to find a SRM manual, however the one on alphalinux.org is
barely sufficient

Phil
