<snip>

> Anyway, once I get the new kernel compiled and put in /boot, and once
> I've edited aboot.conf, how do I get the kernel to boot?
>
> my aboot.conf looks like this:
>
> 0:1/boot/vmlinuz-2.2.14-6.0 root=/dev/hda1
> 1:1/boot/vmlinuz-2.2.16-c1 root=/dev/hda1
>
> If I change the boot_osflags environment variable to 1 in the SRM
> console, the system refuses to mount the root partition, even tho ext2
> is compiled into the kernel.
>

<snip>

Sideband,

Before doing anything make sure that you have BSD disklabels enabled in
your kernel.  Also, I am assuming that you have no trouble booting your
older kernels on the same system.  If this does not work, please give us
more information.

also, your boot_osflag should always be set to  "root=/dev/hda1"
your boot_osfile should now be set to "2/vmlinuz-2.2.16-c1"

your /boot/etc/aboot.conf file should look like the following

1:1/vmlinuz-2.2.14-6.0 root=/dev/hda1
1:2/vmlinuz-2.2.16-c1 root=/dev/hda1

now you will be able to boot by the following
boot yourdev -fl root=/dev/hda1 -fi 2

the -fi 2 means to use 1:2/vmlinuz-2.2.16-c1

good luck!

Mathew Lee
Undergraduate Research Assistant
Oklahoma State University
Mechanical and Aerospace Engieering




