Xavier -

We did a test on our Dual CPU Celerons ( I understand they're a far cry from
your whizbang
alphas) but it's easy to test.  Download NETPIPE and set it up to send a bunch
of one byte messages,
and then plot the latency for each.  When we did this, we still see a severe
problem with MPICH:


     Every 60th message takes on average 150,000 microseconds (MPICH 1.2)
     Every 200th message takes on average 37 microseconds (TCP)
     Every 1450th message takes on average 35 microseconds (LAM 6.3.2)

It smells like the problem that Josip Loncaric ID'd in the 2.0.x kernel days.
Check out
http://www-unix.mcs.anl.gov/mpi/mpich for further info.

Hope that's helpful.
chris.

Xavier Gallez wrote:

> We have installed mpich 1.2.0 on a cluster of Linux Alpha ev67 SMP systems
> (264DP dual CPUs). For some reason, performance is rather disappointing when
> processes running in the same box are to exchange data. We tried two
> different configurations, one with the ch_p4 device and the other with
> ch_shmem. Both gave identical results. So the problems we are facing now
> are:
>
