Bigs:

Look around for a AXPCI133, aka the "Noname" Alpha.  It's an ATX form factor
MB which fits a 166 or 233mhz 221064 Alpha processor, and has expandable
cache.  It has both SCSI and IDE interfaces built in.  They're cheap, one
went on e-Bay last week for $35.  Memory is 72 pin True Parity Simms, which
are a little expensive, but not too bad if you look around.  There are
switches to over clock the processor, but you'll need extra cooling.
Multia's are not that bad, there are 233mhz variants (VX42) which are quite
good.  They don't take up a lot of room on the desktop, and keep you hands
warm on cold nights.  (they have a tendency to get quite warm)
Stay away from AXP150 & AXP 2000 aka 'Jensen', although cheap, and a good
source of parts, they require a lot of work to get Linux working.  Also stay
away from the 3000-xxx (300,400,500...) series, as they have Turbo Channel
architecture which has not been ported to Linux.  I did hear that someone
was going to but have not seen it yet.  These require DEC monitors as they
have a weird plug with BNC connectors.

Good luck.

Tom Crabtree


