Actually the AXPpci33 is a baby AT form factor. Also be careful as the 
UDB (aka Multia) and the AXPpci33 have the same chipset and both are 
often called "noname". 

Out of that generation the AXPpci33 is my favorite, it was well built. 
It has a socket for the CPU (supports up to 300MHz), 3 PCI / 5 ISA, 
supports up to 384MB RAM, has onboard NCR53C875, IDE, serial (make sure 
you get one with the serial cables, they are different...), parallel. 
They also have an optional LED that can be used in Linux thanks to 
Nils Faerber. <http://www.si.unix-ag.org/~nils/LED_module.tar.gz> The 
only real draw back to this board is the "True parity" 72-pin SIMMs 
needed. As they are expensive. If you get one, try to find one with 
PS/2 keyboard and mouse connectors. The other choice is AT-style. While 
it works just as well, AT-style peripherals are more difficult to find. 


HTH,

Ron
