<snip>
> But where oh where is this new kernel located?  I can't put it into
> /boot until I can find it.  Any help or suggestions would be greatly
> appreciated.  I'm sure this is something simple, but alas I'm from the
> x86 world....
<snip>

make sure you are doing

make (whatever config you like    ex. menuconfig, config, etc..)
make dep
make clean    <-- removes your old stuff....etc
make
make boot
make modules
make modules_install


you will find the newly built kernel in /usr/src/linux/arch/alpha/boot
you should copy vmlinuz.gz to /boot
also copy the System.map from the /usr/src/linux directory to the /boot
directory

after completing these steps you will need to reorganize your /boot
directory (get rid of stuff you don't want anymore).

you will also need to edit your /boot/etc/aboot.conf
for more information on the details of the contents of aboot.conf, look at a
milo howto (linuxdoc.org or alphalinux.org)

 commands:

cp /usr/src/linux/arch/alpha/boot/vmlinux.gz /boot
cp /usr/src/linux/System.map /boot
vi /boot/etc/aboot.conf

mine looks like the following
#aboot.conf...please note that all files are relative to the path /boot/
0:1/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda2
0:2/vmlinux.old.gz root=/dev/sda2

doing this allows me to chose my old kernel in case there is a problem with
the newly built one... (boot -fi 2 or boot -fi 1) <--if using SRM, this
assumes that the remaining variables are set....(default boot device and
flags)

good luck

