If any of you have successfully used dump/restore with more than 1 dump 
file to a remote tape, could you please do a dump -v and let me know which 
version you're using?

Here's what happened when I tried it...

Linux system is RH 6.2 on a DS10, using dump (version 0.4b15).
RMT to a DLT4000 drive on a second DS10 running OpenVMS.  Configuration:

  $ mu show/ver
  Process Software MultiNet V4.2 Rev A-X, COMPAQ AlphaServer DS10 466 MHz, OpenVMS AXP V7.2-1

On the Linux box I did this:

  [root@barrel /root]# /sbin/dump -0 -B  1000000 -f seqaxp.cluster:mkb500:/nomount/nostream/norewind/nounload /dev/sda2
  [root@barrel /root]# /sbin/dump -0 -B 10000000 -f seqaxp.cluster:mkb500:/nomount/nostream/norewind/nounload /dev/sda3

and it seemed to do the appropriate thing, putting two dump files from the 
RH 6.2/Alpha Linux box onto the tape in the OpenVMS machine's DLT drive.  
Verification of the first dump file:

  [root@barrel /root]# /sbin/restore -t -f seqaxp.cluster:mkb500:/nomount/nostream/norewind/nounload
  Connection to seqaxp.cluster established.

listed the contents of /dev/sda2.  Good.  So the second command had not
written over the first dump.  Verification of the second dump file: 

  [root@barrel /root]# /sbin/restore -t -f seqaxp.cluster:mkb500:/nomount/nostream/norewind/nounload
  Connection to seqaxp.cluster established.
  /sbin/restore: Tape is not a dump tape

Oops, not good!

Is this a known bug in dump/restore (dump version 0.4b15) or some funny
interaction with RMT?  Or maybe a bug in Multinet on the VMS side? 

Thanks,

David Mathog
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
