Raymond Doetjes wrote:
> Comppiling is not of any value for a benchmark.
> Compiling is first of all pretty IO intensive and second of all strictly
> integer based. Even cross-compilers can't give you an objective test-result.

I disagree.  Try timing your compiles with full optimization vs. no
optimization.  They do roughly the same I/O.  Also, the compiler may do
some FP (e.g. floating-point constant folding) although that is unlikely
to be significant.

I'd hazard a guess that compilation is usually memory bound.

> You need to write becnshmark code that is compiled on both non-optimized and
> optimized compilers for Integer related test, String sorting tests and FP
> tests.

It's all too easy to write microbenchmarks that fit easily within L1
cache, and don't give a good indication of real-world performance.

Benchmarking your own apps is always the best choice, if possible.

