Holger van Koll wrote:
> Are the differences between alpha and i386 RISC/CISC related or is it
> just the chip-design? F.e. does a sparc/powerpc cpu also beat a pentium
> regarding floating-point but not at integer?

There are a lot of decisions that go into a chip design.  RISC and CISC
are just two broad categories that don't adequately explain the
differences.

Some RISC designs didn't have FP at all.  Some are quite good at it. 
The x86 is different than most because it doesn't have a standard FP
register file; it uses a stack (and that is not a CISC characteristic,
btw).

Other characteristics like segmentation can be found either in RISC or
CISC processors.

> And, most interesting to me: Is it true that RISC-code can be
> distributed better on several cpus (in one system) or is scalability
> (regarding only cpu-power) just a matter of application-design and the
> underlying OS?

That probably has nothing to do with chip design.  Some application
codes are more easily parallelizable than others.  Those that can run
loosely coupled parallel tasks may work well on SMP.

The same goes for superscalar designs.  Some codes can benefit more
easily from multiple pipelines and execution units than others.  Though
many RISC processors could not execute out-of-order, that is changing
(witness ev6).

--
Jeff Sturm
jeff.sturm@appnet.com



